The activists framed it as pedestrian safety and privacy, and some of
those concerns were concrete. Underneath ran something older. As
Brian Merchant argues in Blood in the Machine, the original Luddites
werenʼt  ignorant  of  technology;  they  understood  exactly  what  the
new machines would do to their pay and their standing. The cone on
the hood belongs to that lineage.
The campaign has a letterhead
A cone on a robotaxi is the hammer, and the hammer is the half of
the  lineage  everyone  recognizes.  The  other  half  is  quieter,  better
dressed, and far better funded: the organized campaign, run by a
trade body with a budget and a lawyer, against a machine that has just
made its membersʼ skills reproducible.
Itʼs been run at least six times in the last hundred and twenty years,
by serious people with real grievances, and the script barely changes.
Name the defect (the machine has no soul). Predict the death of the
art. Buy the ads. Demand a legal remedy. Lose the argument about
whether the machine exists, which was never the winnable one.
